A second small check concerns progress. Repeating a parser that can successfully return without moving is not an ordinary loop over tokens: it may succeed indefinitely at the same position. In a checked combinator interface, reserve repetition for parsers whose success consumes input, and require recursive calls to be guarded so that direct evaluation cannot revisit the same call indefinitely without consumption. Associativity of the underlying language's concatenation does not establish these properties of an interpreter for grammar expressions.

There is also a genuine ambiguity at a sequence boundary: the expression $\texttt{optional}(a)\,\texttt{optional}(a)$ has two ways to parse the one-token input $a$. The parser could assign it to either optional field. Krishnaswami and Yallop use this as a rejected example. A related test that does not depend on a nullable left operand is $(a\mid ab)b^*$: the input $ab$ splits either after $a$ or after $ab$. Here $b$ both extends an accepted left word to another accepted left word and starts a nonempty right word. That is exactly the kind of overlap checked by the \texttt{FLast} summary.

\section{Static Structure and Dynamic Decisions}

Sergey Baranov's \textit{A Formal Language Processor Implemented in Forth} separates an LL(1) context-free component from other concerns of the language processor, including generating pseudocode. In a combinator library, keeping a similarly visible boundary lets tooling inspect which parts of a grammar are predictive. An applicative sequence has a fixed second parser even if its final value combines both results. A monadic bind can choose the second parser from the first result, for example to read a count and then precisely that many items. This can be useful, but the chosen parser is no longer recoverable from the static combinator structure alone. Offer dynamic operations through an explicit interface instead of claiming the same analysis for every parser.

Krishnaswami and Yallop also use the static information to stage the parser. To test a staged implementation, run the same small grammar through both a reference interpreter and the generated parser. For the factored command above, assert the branch tag, consumed interval, and end-of-input outcome separately. If diagnostics are part of the library's contract, compare expected token sets as well. An algebraic equality of accepted languages cannot justify an optimization that changes an observable commitment point or error location. Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-140} shows how to extend this test beyond a few handwritten strings.

